# LeakSanitizer suppressions — hyperdht-cpp teardown only.
#
# These are DELIBERATELY narrow. Every frame named below is inside the
# dependency; nothing in holesail-cpp is suppressed, so a leak we introduce
# still fails the build. Verified 2026-07-28 against hyperdht-cpp a08cade.
#
# Use with:
#   LSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=test/lsan.supp
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Cancelled queries are never reaped.
#
# hyperdht_query_free() deletes only the wrapper; the underlying query state
# is held alive by an internal completion lambda and released only when that
# lambda fires. hyperdht_query_cancel() does NOT fire it, despite the header
# documenting "on_done fires exactly once per query (natural completion OR
# cancel)". Reproduced standalone: the leak is byte-identical whether or not
# the loop is pumped after cancel, and the done callback never runs.
# Roughly 34 KB per client that is torn down with a lookup in flight.
leak:hyperdht_mutable_get_ex
leak:hyperdht_immutable_get_ex
leak:hyperdht::query::Query

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Baseline teardown leak, ~1237 bytes / 4 allocations per DHT.
#
# Reproducible with a program containing no holesail code at all, following
# the create -> listen -> destroy -> uv_run -> free recipe from hyperdht.h
# verbatim. It leaves one stopped-but-unclosed uv_timer_t in the loop
# (active=0 closing=0 ref=1), which is also why uv_loop_close() cannot
# return 0 after using a DHT.
leak:hyperdht_create
leak:hyperdht::HyperDHT::HyperDHT
